  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating outcomes with probability > 0.5 (i.e., > 1/2) to be considered probable. In both lotteries, the winning probabilities (0.1% and 0.2%) are far below 0.5, so no outcome is considered probable. Therefore, the average number of probable outcomes is zero for both lotteries, and the reason would be indifferent. However, the individual did not consider this threshold or the concept of probable outcomes at all. Instead, the individual justified the choice based solely on the higher probability of winning (0.2% > 0.1%), which is a different criterion. The individual's reasoning does not involve the classification of outcomes as probable or the computation of average probable outcomes. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual used a different criterion — the magnitude of the winning probability — which is not equivalent to the rule in the decision reason. Hence, the decision reason was not applied.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
